= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\Box A$ for the <first-order sentence>[arithmetical sentence] asserting $S$-provability of $A$. If $S\vdash G$, the first derivability condition gives $S\vdash\Box G$. The stipulated <diagonal lemma>[fixed-point equivalence] also gives $S\vdash\neg\Box G$. This contradicts <consistent first-order theory>[consistency]. Conversely an <consistent first-order theory>[inconsistent] classical <first-order theory>[theory] proves every sentence, including $G$. Thus
$$
\boxed{S\nvdash G\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad S\text{ is consistent}.}
$$
Only the direction from an actual proof to its formalized provability is used here. There is no inference from a theorem $\Box G$ to a theorem $G$, and no assumption that every theorem of $S$ is true.

