For <separation of number sets in an arithmetic theory>, a formula separates $A$ from $B$ in $S$ if $n\in A$ implies $S\vdash F(\overline n)$, and $n\in B$ implies $S\vdash\neg F(\overline n)$. No demand is made for numbers outside their <union>. <consistent first-order theory>[Consistency] forces disjointness when such a separator exists.

Let $r$ be the code of the <logical fixed point>[fixed-point] sentence. If $S\vdash R$, then $r$ is in the provable <set>, so separation gives $S\vdash H(\overline r)$. The <diagonal lemma>[fixed-point equivalence] gives its <negation>, a <contradiction>. If $S\vdash\neg R$, then $r$ is in the refutable <set>, so separation gives $S\vdash\neg H(\overline r)$, and the <diagonal lemma>[fixed-point equivalence] gives $S\vdash R$, again a <contradiction>. Thus
$$
\boxed{S\nvdash R\quad\text{and}\quad S\nvdash\neg R.}
$$
The argument is syntactic: it needs only the separating implications and <consistent first-order theory>[consistency], not omega-consistency or general soundness.

