<Gödel second incompleteness theorem>[Gödel's second incompleteness theorem] says that a <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] effectively presented <first-order theory>[arithmetic theory] with an adequate proof predicate cannot prove its <formal consistency statement>. The background strength must support the <diagonal lemma> and the <derivability conditions>, as in the present arithmetic setting.

<Löb's theorem> says
$$
\boxed{S\vdash\Box A\to A\quad\Longrightarrow\quad S\vdash A.}
$$
It is a theorem about derivability for every sentence $A$, not merely an external soundness assertion about the interpretation of $\Box$. Taking $A=\bot$, a proof of <consistent first-order theory>[consistency] gives $S\vdash\Box\bot\to\bot$; Löb then gives $S\vdash\bot$. Thus a <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] $S$ cannot prove <consistent first-order theory>[consistency]. This is how the <Gödel second incompleteness theorem>[second incompleteness theorem] is the contradiction-sentence instance of Löb's more general result.

