An $L$-embedding $\pi:A\to B$ is an <injective> map preserving the interpretations of all constants and functions and preserving and reflecting every relation. Explicitly $\pi(c^A)=c^B$, $\pi(f^A(\bar a))=f^B(\pi\bar a)$, and $A\models R(\bar a)$ if and only if $B\models R(\pi\bar a)$. Injectivity handles <logical equality>. Equivalently it is an <isomorphism of structures>[isomorphism] onto an $L$-substructure of $B$, or a map preserving the truth of all <quantifier-free formulas>. Preserving relations in only one direction would instead give a weaker homomorphism notion, not an <structure embedding>[embedding].

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The relation $A\subseteq B$ means that the domain of $A$ is a <subset> of the domain of $B$ and the inclusion map is an $L$-embedding. Thus it contains all named constants, is closed under every named function, and uses the restrictions of the ambient relations.

The stronger relation $A\preccurlyeq B$ means that for every <first-order formula> $\phi(\bar x)$ and tuple $\bar a$ from $A$,
$$
\boxed{A\models\phi(\bar a)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad B\models\phi(\bar a).}
$$
This includes formulas with <quantifiers> ranging over the respective domains. Elementary inclusion implies ordinary <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructure] inclusion, but the latter need only preserve quantifier-free truth. In particular an existential witness available in $B$ need not lie in an arbitrary <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructure].

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Tarski-Vaught test> says that a <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructure] $A\subseteq B$ is elementary exactly when for every formula $\phi(x,\bar y)$ and tuple $\bar a\in A$, if $B\models\exists x\phi(x,\bar a)$, some $b\in A$ satisfies $B\models\phi(b,\bar a)$. Thus **every existential assertion with parameters from $A$ that has a witness in $B$ already has a witness in $A$**. The matrix may contain <quantifiers>; restricting it to <quantifier-free formulas> would not state the full test.

