= Solution
{parent=iii}

Under the deductively closed convention, an $L$-theory is a <set> of $L$-sentences containing all its first-order logical consequences: if $T\models\phi$, then $\phi\in T$. By completeness this is equivalent to closure under provability. An <axiom> <set> is often called a <first-order theory>[theory] too, with its deductive closure understood. Neither convention by itself requires completeness or satisfiability; an <consistent first-order theory>[inconsistent] deductively closed <first-order theory>[theory] contains every sentence.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A complete satisfiable <first-order theory>[theory] has a <first-order model>[model] and decides every $L$-sentence: for each $\sigma$, exactly one of $\sigma$ and $\neg\sigma$ is a consequence. Under the closed convention, exactly one belongs to $T$. Satisfiability excludes the possibility of both. Equivalently all <first-order model>[models] of the <first-order theory>[theory] are <elementarily equivalent>, even though they need not be isomorphic or have equal <cardinality>.

