<Existential sentences> are preserved under extensions: witnesses remain present and quantifier-free truth is unchanged by an <structure embedding>[embedding]. Therefore any existential axiomatization implies the specified extension-closure property.

For <existential axiomatization from extension closure>, let $E$ be all existential consequences of $T$, and suppose $A\models E$. We claim that $T\cup\operatorname{Th}_\forall(A)$ is satisfiable. Otherwise compactness supplies finitely many <universal sentences> $\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_m$ true in $A$ such that $T\models\neg(\theta_1\land\cdots\land\theta_m)$. The finite <conjunction> is universal after renaming <bound variables>, so its <negation> is existential. It belongs to $E$ but is false in $A$, a <contradiction>.

The preceding universal-theory <structure embedding>[embedding] result now yields $B\models T$ and $A^*\equiv A$ with $B\subseteq A^*$. By the assumed extension closure, $A^*\models T$; <elementary equivalence> implies $A\models T$. Thus $E$ axiomatizes exactly the <first-order model>[models] of $T$, proving **existential axiomatizability is equivalent to closure under extensions**. This includes the <consistent first-order theory>[inconsistent] case, whose empty <first-order model>[model] class is axiomatized by an existential <contradiction>.

= 5
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <first-order theory>[theory] is $\kappa$-categorical if it has a <first-order model>[model] of <cardinality> $\kappa$ and any two <first-order model>[models] of that <cardinality> are isomorphic. The existence clause prevents vacuous categoricity at cardinalities where there is no <first-order model>[model].

To show that <infinite-cardinal categoricity forces completeness>, suppose $T$ were incomplete. Then some sentence $\sigma$ has both $T+\sigma$ and $T+\neg\sigma$ satisfiable, by completeness of <first-order logic>. Their <first-order model>[models] must be infinite because $T$ has no finite <first-order model>[models]. For an infinite $\kappa\ge|L|$, the upward and downward <Löwenheim-Skolem theorems> produce a <first-order model>[model] of each extension of <cardinality> exactly $\kappa$: pass to an elementary submodel of size at most $\max(|L|,\aleph_0)$ and then to the desired size, or reduce a larger <first-order model>[model] to that size. Categoricity makes these two <first-order model>[models] isomorphic, but one satisfies $\sigma$ and the other its <negation>. <Contradiction>. Therefore **$T$ is complete**. If language size is defined without automatically including $\aleph_0$, the infinite-cardinal qualification is explicit; the assumed existence of a $\kappa$-sized <first-order model>[model] and absence of finite <first-order model>[models] already force it here.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $F_n$ be the formulas whose <free variables> are among $x_1,\ldots,x_n$. Define $\phi\,E_n(T)\,\psi$ when $T\models\forall\bar x(\phi\leftrightarrow\psi)$. The <n-variable Lindenbaum algebra> quotient is a <Boolean algebra>, with meet, join and complement induced by <conjunction>, disjunction and <negation>; its zero and one are <contradiction> and truth classes. Its order is $[\phi]\le[\psi]$ exactly when $T\models\forall\bar x(\phi\to\psi)$.

The <Ryll-Nardzewski theorem> states that for a <complete theory> with infinite <first-order model>[models] in a <countable> language, the following are equivalent: the <first-order theory>[theory] is countably categorical; for every finite $n$ there are only finitely many complete $n$-types over the empty <set>; and every <Boolean algebra> $F_n/E_n(T)$ is finite. Equivalently each such type is isolated. The countable-language and completeness hypotheses are essential parts of this formulation.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For each fixed finite tuple length $n$, use the finite <conjunction>
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{LI}_n(\bar x)=\bigwedge_{\bar a\in K^n\setminus\{0\}}\left(\sum_{j=1}^n\lambda_{a_j}(x_j)\ne0\right).}
$$
It asserts that no nontrivial <scalar multiplication>[scalar] combination vanishes, exactly <linear independence>. It is an $L$-formula because the <field> is finite and every <scalar multiplication> is named. <scalar multiplication>[Scalar coefficients] are indices of <function symbols>, not variables ranging over a separate <field> sort. There is no single finite formula with arbitrarily many <free variables>; the assertion is a formula for each prescribed finite $n$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the vector-space <axioms>: addition is an <abelian group> operation with identity zero; $\lambda_1(x)=x$; $\lambda_a\lambda_b(x)=\lambda_{ab}(x)$; $\lambda_a(x+y)=\lambda_a(x)+\lambda_a(y)$; and $\lambda_{a+b}(x)=\lambda_a(x)+\lambda_b(x)$, with $\lambda_0(x)=0$. <Group> inverses may be expressed by $\lambda_{-1}$, so no extra inverse symbol is needed.

Add the dimension-unbounded scheme
$$
\boxed{D_n:\exists x_1\cdots\exists x_n\operatorname{LI}_n(\bar x)\qquad(n\ge1).}
$$
Over a <finite field>, a finite-dimensional space has finitely many <elements>, while every infinite-dimensional space is infinite. Hence this scheme axiomatizes exactly the infinite underlying <vector spaces> requested. In this finite-field case the vector-space <axiom> list itself is finite; the infinitude of the additional <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] scheme is substantive.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** <Universal sentences> are preserved under <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructures]. Every infinite $K$-vector space has its zero subspace as an $L$-substructure, since zero is named and every <scalar multiplication> preserves it. This one-element structure is not infinite and is not a <first-order model>[model] of $T$. Thus the <first-order model>[model] class is not closed under <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructures] and has no universal axiomatization, even relative to the vector-space <axioms>.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**No.** If a finite <conjunction> $\phi$ axiomatized the infinite spaces, it would follow from the vector-space <axioms> together with all the $D_n$. Compactness would make it follow from finitely many of those <axioms> and <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] requirements. Let $N$ be at least every <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] mentioned there. The finite-dimensional space $K^N$ satisfies all vector-space <axioms> and those finitely many $D_n$, hence would satisfy $\phi$. But it is a <finite set> and so cannot be a <first-order model>[model] of the purported axiomatization. This <contradiction> proves failure of <finite axiomatizability>[finite axiomatizability], including <finite axiomatizability>[finite axiomatizability] relative to <vector spaces>.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For <quantifier elimination for infinite underlying vector spaces>, we first justify that formulas cannot make finer distinctions than their linear-equation data. In any infinite underlying <vector space>, eliminate an existential variable from a disjunction of <conjunctions> of <linear equations> and inequations. If an equation has a nonzero <scalar multiplication>[coefficient] of that variable, solve for it and substitute. If none does, the inequations forbid only finitely many values, leaving an available value in the infinite domain; the remaining equations concern only the other variables. Terms are <linear combinations>, so induction gives <quantifier elimination> for $T$ and completeness, since closed terms reduce to zero.

For one variable there are two possible <complete types>, zero and nonzero. Both are isolated, and every definable <set> is their <union>. Thus
$$
\boxed{F_1/E_1(T)\cong\mathcal P(\{1,2\}),\qquad |F_1/E_1(T)|=4.}
$$
For <types of pairs of vectors over a finite field>, the <linear relation> kernel $\{(a,b)\in K^2:ax+by=0\}$ determines the <complete type>. If $q=|K|$, the possibilities are one rank-zero pair, $q+1$ rank-one directions, and one rank-two independent type. Concretely these are $(0,0)$; $x\ne0$ with $y=\lambda_a(x)$ for each $a\in K$; $x=0,y\ne0$; and the independent pairs. Their isolating formulas are finite because $K$ is finite. Hence
$$
\boxed{F_2/E_2(T)\cong\mathcal P(\{1,\ldots,q+3\}),\qquad |F_2/E_2(T)|=2^{q+3}.}
$$
Every class is the <union> of a selection of these <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms]. <Basis extension> also shows that pairs with the same relation kernel are conjugate by a <linear isomorphism>[linear automorphism] within any infinite-dimensional <first-order model>[model].

= vi
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Every <vector space> has a <vector space basis>[basis] and is classified up to <linear isomorphism> by its <dimension of a vector space>[dimension]. Over a <finite field>, a vector is a finite <linear combination> of <vector space basis>[basis] <elements>. If the <vector space basis>[basis] has infinite <cardinality> $\lambda$, the <set> of finite such combinations has <cardinality> $\lambda$: there are at least $\lambda$ <basis vectors> and at most a <countable union> of finite powers of $\lambda$, each of size $\lambda$. Thus the underlying <cardinality> of an infinite space equals its <dimension of a vector space>[dimension].

Any two <first-order model>[models] of <cardinality> $\kappa\ge\aleph_0$ therefore have <vector space basis>[bases] of <cardinality> $\kappa$. A <bijection> between their <vector space basis>[bases] extends uniquely to an $L$-isomorphism. A space with that <vector space basis>[basis] size exists, giving the required existence clause as well. Hence **$T$ is categorical in every infinite cardinal**.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <cardinality of a vector space> is central: here “infinite” must continue to refer to the underlying <set>. Over a countably infinite <field>, every nonzero finite-dimensional space is already infinite. Thus the <first-order model>[model] class now consists of all nonzero $K$-vector spaces, not only infinite-dimensional ones.

Since <independence of a pair is not definable over an infinite field>, for (i), independence of a single vector is still $x\ne0$, but independence of tuples of length at least two is not definable by one formula in this one-sorted language. <Quantifier elimination> follows from the linear-equation argument in the preceding part for all these infinite domains. Any candidate two-variable formula therefore involves only finitely many <linear equations>. Take an independent pair, and compare it with $(x,\lambda_a x)$, where $x\ne0$ and $a\in K$ avoids the finitely many ratios making one of those nontrivial equations vanish. Both pairs satisfy the same listed atomic formulas, contradicting a proposed definition of independence. Longer tuple independence would define pair independence by existentially completing a pair inside an infinite-dimensional <first-order model>[model], giving the same obstruction.

For (ii), the vector-space <axiom> schema together with $\exists x(x\ne0)$ axiomatizes the infinite underlying spaces. For (iii), there is again no universal axiomatization, because the zero subspace is a <substructure of a first-order structure>[substructure].

For <finite axiomatizability with infinitely many scalar symbols>, in (iv) there is no <finite axiomatizability>[finite axiomatization] in the full language: any finite <axiom> <set> mentions only finitely many <scalar multiplication>[scalar] symbols. In a nonzero <vector space> alter an unused nonzero $\lambda_a$ to send zero to a nonzero vector, retaining all mentioned interpretations. The finite sentences keep their <truth values>, but a vector-space <axiom> is now false. **Relative to the already imposed vector-space <axioms>**, the extra condition is finitely axiomatized by the single nonzero-vector sentence. These absolute and relative answers must be distinguished.

For (v), the one-variable algebra still has two <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms] and four <elements>. In two variables the isolated <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms] are $(0,0)$ and the countably many rank-one directions, represented by $x\ne0,y=\lambda_a x$ and by $x=0,y\ne0$. There is also a nonisolated <complete type> of an independent pair. A formula using finitely many <linear equations> either picks finitely many isolated <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms], or contains that independent type and all but finitely many isolated <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms]. Therefore **$F_2/E_2(T)$ is the <finite-cofinite Boolean algebra>[finite-cofinite Boolean algebra] on a countably <infinite set> of <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms]**, with the independent type the extra nonisolated point in its Stone space. In particular this algebra is countably infinite, rather than the full <power set> of its <atom of a Boolean algebra>[atoms].

For (vi), <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] can be $1,2,3,\ldots$ or $\aleph_0$ while the domain is <countable>, producing pairwise nonisomorphic <countable> <first-order model>[models]. Thus $T$ is not countably categorical. For an uncountable $\kappa$, a <vector space> over this <countable> <field> has <cardinality> $\kappa$ exactly when its <dimension of a vector space>[dimension] is $\kappa$, since in general a nonzero space has size $\max(|K|,\dim V)$ when that maximum is infinite. <vector space basis>[Basis] <bijections> then give **categoricity in every uncountable cardinal**. <Quantifier elimination> also shows that these different positive <dimension of a vector space>[dimensions] are <elementarily equivalent>; independence of an arbitrarily long tuple is not being silently treated as definable over this infinite <field>.

= 6
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A complete $n$-type over $T$ is a maximal <set> $p(\bar x)$ of formulas in $n$ variables <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] with $T$; it decides each such formula or its <negation>. A <partial type> drops maximality. A tuple realizes the type if it satisfies every member. A <complete type> is principal, or isolated, if some formula $\theta(\bar x)$ <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] with $T$ implies every member of the type modulo $T$; then $\theta$ itself belongs to that type.

The <omitting types theorem> says that in a <countable> <first-order language>, a <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] <first-order theory>[theory] has a <countable> <first-order model>[model] omitting a specified nonprincipal type; more generally a <countable> family of nonprincipal types can be omitted simultaneously. In the present complete-theory setting it applies directly to complete nonisolated types. “Omitted” means that no tuple in the <first-order model>[model] realizes all formulas of the type, even though each finite <subset> is compatible with the <first-order theory>[theory].

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <first-order model>[model] is prime if it has an <elementary embedding> into every <first-order model>[model] of $T$. A <first-order model>[model] is atomic if every finite tuple realizes an isolated <complete type> over the empty <set>.

If $A$ is prime, the downward <Löwenheim-Skolem theorem> gives a <countable> <first-order model>[model] $M$ of $T$, and an <elementary embedding> $A\to M$ makes $A$ <countable>. If a tuple of $A$ realized a nonisolated type, the <omitting types theorem> would give a <first-order model>[model] of $T$ omitting that type. The prime <structure embedding>[embedding] into it would preserve the tuple's full type and realize the <omitted type>, a <contradiction>. Thus a <prime model> is <countable> and atomic.

Conversely enumerate a <countable> <atomic model> as $a_0,a_1,\ldots$, and take any $B\models T$. Build finite <partial elementary map>[partial elementary maps] into $B$. Suppose the current tuple $\bar a$ maps to $\bar b$. Choose a formula $\theta(\bar x,y)$ isolating the type of $(\bar a,a_n)$. The assertion $\exists y\theta(\bar a,y)$ holds in $A$, so its counterpart holds at $\bar b$ in $B$ by partial elementarity. Choose a witness $b_n$. Since $\theta$ isolates the entire extended tuple type, the extended map is elementary on its finite domain; its equality formulas ensure distinct <elements> remain distinct. The <union> is a total <elementary embedding> $A\to B$. The empty initial tuple works because $T$ is complete. Hence
$$
\boxed{A\text{ is prime}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad A\text{ is countable and atomic}.}
$$

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

An $\aleph_0$-saturated <first-order model>[model] realizes every <complete type> in finitely many variables over every finite parameter <subset> of itself. It suffices equivalently to require one-variable types and then realize finite tuples successively. The definition puts no countability restriction on the size of the <first-order model>[model].

For any <first-order model>[model] $M$, collect all <complete types> over its finite parameter <sets>. Add to its <elementary diagram>, for each such type, a new tuple of constants satisfying all that type's formulas. Any finite selection of these requirements is realizable in $M$: each finite type fragment has its existential closure true there, since it is <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] with the <complete theory> of the corresponding named finite tuple. Distinct new tuples have no additional mutual restrictions. Compactness gives an <elementary extension> realizing all these types simultaneously.

Start from any <first-order model>[model] $M_0\models T$ and apply this construction repeatedly to obtain $M_0\preccurlyeq M_1\preccurlyeq\cdots$, with each successor realizing all types over finite <subsets> of its predecessor. The <elementary chain theorem>[elementary-chain theorem] makes $M_\omega=\bigcup_nM_n$ a <first-order model>[model] of $T$ with every stage elementary in it. A finite parameter <set> $A\subseteq M_\omega$ lies in some $M_n$. Every type over $A$ <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] with $M_\omega$ is <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] with $M_n$, since the expanded complete <first-order theory>[theories] at those parameters agree by elementarity. It is realized in $M_{n+1}$, hence in the <union>. Therefore **an $\aleph_0$-saturated <first-order model>[model] always exists**, possibly with uncountable <cardinality>.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For <cut types over the named rational order>, choose an irrational real cut, for example $r=\sqrt2$, and form
$$
p_r(x)=\{c_q<x:q\in\mathbb Q,\ q<r\}\cup\{x<c_q:q\in\mathbb Q,\ q>r\}.
$$
Every finite <subset> is realized by a rational between its finite lower and <upper bounds>, so compactness makes it a <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] <partial type>; <quantifier elimination for dense linear orders without endpoints> makes these comparisons determine a <complete type> in the language naming all rationals. No rational realizes the cut, so $Q^+$ omits it.

Add a new constant realizing this type and apply compactness, followed by downward Löwenheim-Skolem in the <countable> expanded language. This gives a <countable> <first-order model>[model] of $T$ realizing it. An <isomorphism of structures>[isomorphism] in $L_{\mathbb Q}$ fixes all named rationals and preserves realization of the cut, so it cannot be isomorphic to $Q^+$. Thus **$T$ is not countably categorical**. Omission by itself would need this additional realization-and-countable-model argument to establish noncategoricity.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No infinite cardinal is categorical for this <first-order theory>[theory]**, as described by <no categorical cardinal for the complete named rational diagram>. For an arbitrary infinite $\kappa$, choose a dense linear order $I_\kappa$ without endpoints of size $\kappa$; for example the <lexicographic order> on $\kappa\times\mathbb Q$ works, since every rational block is dense and has no endpoints.

Construct one order by inserting $I_\kappa$ into a fixed irrational cut of the named rational order. Every element of this order is bounded above by some named rational. Construct a second order by putting an $I_\kappa$ tail above all named rationals, as the ordered sum $\mathbb Q+I_\kappa$. Both are dense linear orders without endpoints, both have <cardinality> $\kappa$, and both contain the named rational copy as an <elementary substructure> by <quantifier elimination>. Hence both satisfy its <elementary diagram of a structure>[complete diagram] $T$.

They are not isomorphic over the named constants: the second has an element greater than every named rational, and the first has none. This property need not be expressible by a single sentence in the infinite language to be an <isomorphism of structures>[isomorphism] invariant. Finite cardinalities have no <first-order model>[models] at all because the constants name infinitely many distinct <elements>, so the existence clause excludes categoricity there too.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Yes: $Q^+$ is prime.** In any <first-order model>[model] of its <elementary diagram of a structure>[complete diagram], map each rational to the interpretation of its named constant. The diagram ensures this is an <structure embedding>[embedding], and <quantifier elimination for dense linear orders without endpoints> makes it elementary. Alternatively every finite tuple of $Q^+$ has its <complete type> isolated by the <conjunction> equating its coordinates to their named rational constants. Thus it is a <countable> <atomic model>, and the earlier prime-model equivalence applies. A <prime model> may omit many types; primeness is not saturation.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

There are $2^{\aleph_0}$ distinct irrational cuts of the named rational order. Each defines a <consistent first-order theory>[consistent] complete one-type as above, already over the empty additional parameter <set>, since the rational constants are part of the language. Different cuts cannot be realized by the same element: a named rational between two cuts gives contradictory required inequalities.

An $\aleph_0$-saturated <first-order model>[model] must realize every one of these types, so it has at least $2^{\aleph_0}$ <elements>. Consequently **there is no <countable> $\aleph_0$-saturated <first-order model>[model] of $T$**. This does not contradict existence of a <saturated model> from the preceding construction, whose size was unrestricted, or existence of the <countable> <prime model>, whose requirement is different. No continuum-hypothesis assumption is involved.
